def add(id,section,title,scans,lines,kind,body,comparison,terms,confidence=''):
 NOTES.append(dict(id=id,section=section,title=title,scans=scans,lines=lines,kind=kind,body=body,comparison=comparison,terms=terms,confidence=confidence))
def save_manifest():
 (ROOT/'records/research_note_manifest.json').write_text(json.dumps(NOTES,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')

A='I. Programme, base change and analytic comparison'
add('N01',A,'The opening programme has a reported later continuation','002','3–9','unpursued implication',r'''“Nous prendrons comme prétexte la théorie des fonctions $L$” introduces three distinct tasks: torsion-coefficient duality with Künneth and Lefschetz, an $l$-adic extension, and applications to $L$-functions. The later package reports a renewed preference for completing the formalism and an unfinished passage from finite levels to adic traces. These scans supply an explicit programme statement, but scan order alone does not date its composition or show that the adic task was completed.''',C+' N20/N21 (122), N24 (128), N40 (128,132,146): **adds** the three-part outline and **restates** the programme link; bears on register **MPL_33_098-146 N20, N21, N40** (all first-pass leads), and README **S19**.', ['fonctions','adic','formalisme','rationalit'])
add('N02',A,'Strict localization is a distinct input to the fibre calculus','004','7–41','unpursued implication',r'''The margin says that strict localization and the ensuing fibre calculation have no “analogue complet” for ordinary topological spaces, while the text uses a conservative family of geometric fibres and a strict-local section formula. A useful comparison should distinguish that special étale localization mechanism from the general fact that stalks detect sheaf isomorphisms. The ordinary product-of-stalks construction on 041–043 is therefore a separate foundational source, not automatically the topological counterpart claimed missing here.''',C+' N18 (120, boundary stalks) and N31 (138,140, pointwise Tor bounds) **differ** in their use of stalks; this **adds** a localization-specific question to README **S19**, with no same-point result in '+R+'.',['localisation','strict','stalk','fibre'])
add('N03',A,'Proper and smooth base change retain different hypotheses','004,006','004:44; 006:3–16','unproved claim',r'''The fundamental theorem says that proper direct images of torsion sheaves “commute à tt changement de base,” whereas the following NB drops properness for a smooth base change, keeping the original morphism quasi-compact and quasi-separated and the coefficients prime to residual characteristics. The modern formulations in [Stacks 095T](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/095T) and [0EYU](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0EYU) confirm those two distinct regimes. The manuscript's geometric-fibre and separably-closed-field examples are applications of the stated inputs, not proofs of either theorem on these leaves.''',C+' N12–N14 (114,116,118,120): **adds** the explicit hypotheses behind later projection/Künneth arguments; bears on register **MPL_33_098-146 §76** (first-pass comparison lead), without closing the later functoriality check.',['base change','changement de base','proper','smooth'])
add('N04',A,'The analytic comparison is enlarged and its categorical version deferred','006','18–30','unproved claim',r'''The cohomological comparison with $X^{\mathrm{tr}}$ is asserted to be an isomorphism, and the relative sentence replaces cancelled “propre” by “de type fini.” The GAGA margin goes further to an equivalence of constructible torsion-sheaf categories and ends with the doubtful reading “à rédiger.” Both the precise constructibility convention and the resolution-of-singularities qualification need collation; the cancelled analytic-local-ring paragraph does not itself complete that argument.''',C+' N42 (099) is an analytic operator-algebra sheet and **differs** from this comparison; the present material **adds** an analytic branch to README **S19**, with no same-point theorem in '+R+'.',['GAGA','analytic','analytique','constructible','resolution'])
add('N05',A,'A scope test for the categorical GAGA margin','006','28','own idea',r'''If “faisceaux constructibles analytiques” includes arbitrary complex-analytic stratifications, take $X=\mathbb A^2_{\mathbb C}$ and a nonzero finite constant sheaf on the closed analytic graph $Z=\{w=e^z\}$, extended by zero. It is constructible for the analytic partition $Z\sqcup(X^{\mathrm{an}}\setminus Z)$, but its support is not algebraically constructible: the graph is Zariski dense and contains no nonempty Zariski open subset, whereas algebraic constructibility uses finite algebraic strata ([Stacks 03RW](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03RW)). This tests a broad reading of the margin and suggests recovering an algebraic-stratification convention; it does not refute an equivalence already restricted to such strata.''',C+' N50–N51 (135) and N54 (145) provide **different** conditional scope tests; this **adds** a new GAGA-specific test, not a repetition of their examples, and register **MPL_33_098-146 N54** remains an unreviewed methodological lead.',['analytic','constructible','stratif','scope','criterion'], 'high for the conditional support obstruction: no polynomial relation P(z,e^z)=0 can hold, by comparing the largest exponential power along the positive real axis; the intended manuscript convention is unresolved')
add('N06',A,'The two relative analytic comparison maps are explicitly left to prove','008','8–19','self-flagged: a step he himself marks as unverified',r'''“À prouver que ce sont des isomorphismes” applies to both the absolute cohomology comparison and the relative higher-direct-image comparison displayed immediately above it. A reconstruction has to define the relative algebraic/analytic categories over the analytic base and prove compatibility with restriction before treating the two statements as interchangeable. No proof is supplied on this page.''',C+' N42 (099) **differs** in subject, and N12 (114) concerns another comparison map; this **adds** an explicit unfinished task to the standing inventory represented by register **MPL_33_098-146 §65**, with no same-point result.',['isomorphismes','analytic','comparison','GAGA'])
add('N07',A,'The rigid-analytic extension is an expectation','008','21','conjecture',r'''The margin says “Devrait aussi se généraliser au contexte rigide-analytique,” extending the proposed relative comparison beyond complex analytic geometry. Its hypotheses and even the intended rigid-analytic realization functor are not specified here. This should be indexed as an expectation requiring a precise formulation, not as an already proved rigid comparison theorem.''',C+' has no same-point rigid-analytic item or matching scan; its N42 (099) **differs** despite the analytic vocabulary, so this **adds** a distinct conjectural branch to README **S19** and has no settled counterpart in '+R+'.',['rigide','rigid','analytic','GAGA'])
add('N08',A,'The neighborhood-limit application is conditional','008','23–31','unpursued implication',r'''“on trouverait alors” makes the neighborhood-limit formula depend on the preceding unproved analytic comparison. The complement example then proposes computing the analytic higher direct image within algebraic geometry. The passage needs both a continuity argument for the shrinking neighborhoods and the exact coefficient/support convention; it is not established merely by writing a limit sign.''',C+' N24 (128, finite-level-to-adic traces) is a **different** limit problem; this **adds** an analytic-local application rather than settling that later expectation, and register **MPL_33_098-146 N40** is only an unreviewed related compatibility lead.',['limite','limit','neigh','analytic','coefficient'])
add('N09',A,'Smooth proper specialization supplies the later local-constancy example','010,048','010:2; 048:12','unpursued implication',r'''The specialization assertion for $f$ “propre et lisse” gives locally free higher direct images for the stated prime-to-characteristic locally free torsion coefficients. It supplies an internal explanation for “p.ex. $f$ propre lisse!” in the later duality application. This connection must keep the coefficient hypotheses: a statement about flatness of a pullback functor is not by itself a statement that all finite coefficient modules are perfect.''',C+' N19 (112,120, locally constant projection variant) **differs** in the actual formula but is related in hypotheses; N08 (108,112) supplies the reported coefficient warning, so the present pages **add** a concrete example under register **MPL_33_098-146 N08** (first-pass lead).',['special','locally constant','loc.','Tor','proper'])
add('N10',A,'Absolute, affine and relative cohomological bounds must be separated','010','4–18','unproved claim',r'''The manuscript gives the $2\dim X$ bound over a separably closed field, an affine improvement to $\dim X$, and warns that this “peut donner des idées fausses.” These are absolute cohomological assertions, distinct from the relative bound for a proper direct image and from a Tor-amplitude estimate for a complex. The strict/non-strict sign at the first bound remains a frozen reading doubt; later mathematical comparisons do not decide its ink.''',C+' N09 (110, ordinary relative dimension) and N32/N33 (138–142, compact-support amplitude) **differ** in scope; this **adds** the absolute starting point beside register **MPL_33_098-146 N30, §76** (first-pass leads).',['dimension','affine','cohomological','amplitude','bound'])
add('N11',A,'Supported sections supply an elementary precursor to exchange duality','012','4–41','unpursued implication',r'''Under “Supports,” the page identifies sheaves on a closed complement with sheaves trivial or zero on its open complement, then expresses supported cohomology through Ext and local cohomology. This isolates the closed-support mechanism needed later for duality across an immersion. The references “SGA 1962” and “Notes Hartshorne 1961” are source pointers, not a proof of all displayed identifications on this page.''',C+' N01 (098, closed-immersion calculation and D exchanging operations): **adds** the support-functor setup, without proving the full exchange identities; bears on register **MPL_33_098-146 N01** (first-pass lead).',['support','closed','immersion','Ext','Hartshorne'])
add('N12',A,'The local fundamental class is explicitly requested before its construction','012,022,024','012:57; 022:15–26; 024:4–22','self-flagged: a step he himself marks as unverified',r'''The margin on 012 asks “Donner [définition] de la classe fondamentale … locale,” with the bracketed word uncertain in the frozen reading. Scans 022–024 subsequently give the divisor/Kummer construction and its higher-codimension cup-product extension. Thus the request is taken up within this package, while independence of the chosen local equations still remains a separate proof obligation.''',C+' N47 (123, relative divisors in a reused source) **differs**, rather than supplying the missing definition; this **adds** an internally continued task to register **MPL_33_098-146 §65** (standing-question lead).',['fundament','divisor','diviseur','Kummer','definition'])

B='II. Purity, finiteness, fundamental classes and geometric trace'
add('N13',B,'Absolute purity is a boxed problem with stated qualifications','012,014,026','012:44–57; 014:18–20; 026:20–32','self-flagged: a step he himself marks as unverified',r'''The boxed “Pb Pureté ``absolue''” changes the desired hypothesis from smoothness toward regularity, but the connecting wording is explicitly uncertain. The same paragraph attaches “pour bons premiers,” equal characteristic and availability of resolution, while 026 later lists three settings for the vanishing used to construct cycle classes. These are evidence of the author's intended purity programme and dependencies, not an unrestricted proof of absolute purity.''',C+' N20 (122, curve application “sans résolution des singularités”) **differs** in object and scope; the current scans **add** the purity dependency under register **MPL_33_098-146 N20** (first-pass lead), with no same-point settled result.',['pure','purity','résolution','resolution','regular'])
add('N14',B,'The relative and normal-crossing extensions are only announced','014','20–24','unproved claim',r'''“Il y a aussi un th de pureté relatif sur une base quelconque” and “Extension aux div. à croisements normaux” announce broader statements without their formulations or proofs. A useful reconstruction would first specify the support strata and which residue-characteristic restrictions survive before applying the displayed pure-codimension formula to a union of divisors. Neither phrase should be treated as a blanket extension to arbitrary singular supports.''',C+' N47 (123) discusses a different reused-source notion of relative divisors and **differs**; the present announcements **add** two source dependencies to README **S19**, with no same-point theorem in '+R+'.',['divisor','diviseur','normal','purity','relative'])
add('N15',B,'The twist warning is part of the trace normalization','014,032','014:9–16; 032:6–10','unpursued implication',r'''“Il n'faut surtout pas les confondre” distinguishes $\mu_n$ from a chosen constant $\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z$ sheaf, and the page records how a primitive-root choice changes its tensor-power trivialization. The orientation sheaf $T_{X/Y}$ on 032 therefore carries descent information which should remain visible in any comparison of local classes and global trace. Choosing coordinates can calculate the map, but cannot silently replace that twist everywhere by constant coefficients.''',C+' N29 (134,136, abstract contraction) is a **related but different** trace construction; this **adds** a geometric normalization input beside register **MPL_33_098-146 N29** (first-pass lead), rather than identifying the two traces.',['twist','Kummer','duality','contraction','coefficient'])
add('N16',B,'A primitive-root rescaling needs a unit if it is to remain primitive','014','16','own idea',r'''The phrase “$\lambda\in\mathbf Z/n\mathbf Z$” occurs in the rescaling of a primitive section. For $n>1$, a generator of the cyclic module remains a generator after multiplication by $\lambda$ only when $\lambda$ is a unit; taking $\lambda=0$ already destroys the stated trivialization. This is a conditional scope check on the phrase, not an insertion of a missing unit sign into the transcription.''',C+' N23 (126, finite coefficient rings) and N25 (126–132, a different coefficient example) **differ**; this **adds** a primitive-section scope question, with register **MPL_33_098-146 N23/N25** remaining first-pass leads.',['primitive','unit','coefficient','finite ring','Kummer'],'high for the elementary cyclic-module check; whether invertibility is an inherited convention for this rescaling is unresolved')
add('N17',B,'Proper constructibility does not supply all later finiteness conditions','016','8–12','unproved claim',r'''The theorem says that proper direct images of constructible torsion sheaves are “constr.” and derives finite cohomology over a separably closed field, without a proof here. The later trace programme separately asks for finite Tor dimension, so this earlier finiteness statement must not be enlarged into that stronger assertion. [Stacks 0GL0](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GL0) gives a precise modern constructibility comparison for $Rf_!$ for a separated morphism of finite presentation between quasi-compact, quasi-separated schemes, with a Noetherian coefficient ring and the stated torsion-complex scope; it applies in all degrees when the coefficient ring is torsion.''',C+' N25 (126–132) and N30 (136,140,142) **add later distinctions** between constructibility, pseudo-coherence and Tor bounds; this page supplies an input under register **MPL_33_098-146 N25/N30** (unreviewed leads), without rederiving their examples.',['constructib','finit','pseudo','Tor','proper'])
add('N18',B,'The nonproper constructibility question proposes an immersion reduction','016,018','016:14; 018:2','self-flagged: a step he himself marks as unverified',r'''The doubtful heading “[Question] de la généralisation” continues with “à des $f$ de type fini au lieu de $f$ propre” and proposes reducing to an open immersion. Resolution of singularities plus purity is then invoked, with difficult good-prime/good-scheme qualifications retained as doubts. This is a proposed route whose exact hypotheses and independence from resolution remain to be assessed, not a uniform nonproper theorem proved on these sheets.''',C+' N15 (118, nonproper Künneth) and N20 (122, curves without resolution) **differ** in their assertions but **add** related duality/immersion methods; bears on register **MPL_33_098-146 N15/N20** (first-pass leads), with no closure of this constructibility question.',['nonproper','immersion','resolution','constructib','pure'])
add('N19',B,'Independence of compactification precedes canonical coherence','014,016,018,020','014:26–29; 016:2; 018:6–12; 020:2','unproved claim',r'''After a cancelled start on 014–016, the construction resumes with extension by zero followed by proper direct image and says it is “indépendant du choix de la factor.” by base change. Independence up to isomorphism still needs compatible comparison maps for common refinements if it is to support the composition and base-change identities on 042. The detailed choice-independence argument in [Stacks 0F7J](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0F7J) is a concrete S19 comparison target, without turning the cancelled start into a mathematical retraction.''',C+' N11 (112) and N12 (114) **add later** projection and explicitly unfinished derived functoriality checks; this **adds** their compactification input under register **MPL_33_098-146 §65/§76** (first-pass leads).',['compactif','independen','functor','fonctor','base change'])
add('N20',B,'The quasi-finite vanishing needs its degree range recovered','020','4,17','own idea',r'''Taken literally for $i=0$, “Si $f$ quasi-fini … alors les $R^i_!f_*(F)=0$” contradicts the same page's definition: take $f=\mathrm{id}$ and $F\ne0$, giving $R^0_!f_*F=F$. The finite-map comparison in [Stacks 03QP](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03QP) explicitly restricts higher-direct-image vanishing to positive degree. The page therefore needs its missing or inherited quantifier checked, while the frozen formula stays unchanged.''',C+' N01 (098) uses another degree-zero immersion calculation and **differs**; this **adds** an internal scope test to README **S19**, with no same-point result in '+R+'.',['quasi','finite','vanish','degree','immersion'],'high: the identity map and the same-page degree-zero definition already give the conditional counterexample')
add('N21',B,'Étale trace is a special case to compare with geometric trace','020,032,034','020:20–34; 032:10–12; 034:3–20','unpursued implication',r'''“Si $f$ étale, on a un hom can.” introduces a degree-zero trace and covariance before the pure-relative-dimension trace is characterized. [Stacks 03SH](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03SH) identifies étale trace with the adjunction counit and, for a split finite étale map, with summation over the sheets. Agreement of this concrete map with the later smooth trace and with the endomorphism trace from duality is a compatibility question, not a consequence of sharing the word “trace.”''',C+' N29 (134,136) and N37 (146) **add** contraction and symmetry for a different trace construction; this supplies a geometric special case under register **MPL_33_098-146 N29/N37** (first-pass leads).',['trace','counit','adjunction','symmetry','contraction'])
add('N22',B,'The divisor class comes from units and the Kummer boundary','022,024','022:8–26; 024:4–12','unproved claim',r'''The text makes $d\varphi$ a “section canonique” by passing from a regular equation to its class modulo units, then applies the Kummer boundary to define the finite-coefficient local class. It asserts Zariski locality and base-change compatibility when the pulled-back equation remains regular. A proof should check that the quotient, boundary and restriction maps agree, rather than replacing “admissible” base change by every square.''',C+' N47 (123, relative meromorphic divisors in a reused source) **differs**, and N12 (114) concerns another functorial comparison; this **adds** a local-class construction to README **S19**, with no same-point theorem in '+R+'.',['Kummer','divisor','diviseur','boundary','base change'])
add('N23',B,'The positive relative-dimension wording is a reading question','022','8','reading question',r'''The frozen phrase is “intersection complète de dim relative $1$” for $Y=V(\varphi)\to X$, with no minus sign supplied. The following regular-equation construction and the codimension-$d$ discussion on 024 invite a codimension-one or relative-dimension-minus-one interpretation, but neither gives permission to change the written sign. This is a collation question about terminology or an omitted sign, distinct from proving the local-class construction.''',C+' N47 (123) again concerns **different** relative-divisor definitions and supplies no matching line; this **adds** a source-level question to README **S19**, with no resolving entry in '+R+'.',['relative','codimension','divisor','complete intersection'])
add('N24',B,'Choice independence for higher-codimension classes is not proved here','024','12–22','unproved claim',r'''After defining the class as a product of divisor classes, the page says “on prouve que cette section ne dépend pas du choix des $Y_i$, donc se définit globalement.” This is the substantive step needed to pass from a chosen local regular sequence to an intrinsic globally glued class. The page gives the construction and the assertion, but not the change-of-generators argument.''',C+' N37 (146, derived symmetry) supplies a **different** sign/compatibility problem, relevant to reordering but not a proof of all changes of regular sequence; this **adds** an unfinished local-class verification beside register **MPL_33_098-146 N37** (first-pass lead).',['cup','symmetry','independen','choix','regular sequence'])
save_manifest()
add('N25',B,'A concrete route for testing regular-sequence independence','024,026','024:12–22; 026:5–10','own idea',r'''A possible reconstruction is to test invariance first under permutations, multiplication of one equation by a unit, and addition of a multiple of another equation. The even cohomological degree removes a sign for permutations, and the divisor's quotient-by-units construction addresses the second operation; the third operation still needs an argument compatible with the admissible base changes in 026. This isolates a finite list of local checks without claiming that those checks or the resulting gluing have been completed.''',C+' N12 (114, functoriality) and N37 (146, symmetry) **differ** from this regular-sequence problem; the proposal **adds** a specific local verification route beside register **MPL_33_098-146 N37/§65** (first-pass leads).',['regular','symmetry','functorial','base change','gluing'],'medium: the first two operations have explicit mechanisms on these pages, while the generator-replacement and gluing steps remain unproved')
add('N26',B,'The characteristic properties are also a uniqueness claim','026','2–16','unproved claim',r'''The margin calls base-change compatibility, the divisor normalization and the intersection-product rule “propriétés caractéristiques,” then draws an implication to compatibility for variable $n$. To justify this one needs a uniqueness argument reducing a local complete intersection to its divisor factors and checking that coefficient-change maps preserve the normalization. The word “caractéristiques” therefore carries more content than a list of separately valid identities.''',C+' N40 (128,132,146) concerns **different** coefficient compatibility for traces; this **adds** a uniqueness/transition-map input for cycle classes under register **MPL_33_098-146 N40** (first-pass lead), without settling that later limit problem.',['caractér','coefficient','compatib','finite-level','limit'])
add('N27',B,'The adic cycle class gives a distinct finite-level compatibility test','026','13–16','own idea',r'''For the assertion that the “bonne” class lies in local cohomology with $\mathbb Z_\ell(d)$, a useful next test is to form the classes for $n=\ell^r$ and verify the transition maps using the divisor normalization and product rule. This would provide the cycle-class input to an adic construction, while leaving the comparison between inverse limits and cohomology to be justified. It does not duplicate the later reader's matrix-trace calculation, whose remaining problem is compatible geometric perfect models.''',C+' N24 (128) and N40 (128,132,146): **adds** a local-cycle-class test and **differs** in object from the trace-limit test; bears on register **MPL_33_098-146 N40** (unreviewed own-idea lead).',['adic','limite','limit','coefficient','compatible'],'medium: the finite-level normalization is explicit, but the required limit/cohomology comparison has not been proved here')
add('N28',B,'The local-to-global edge isomorphism uses vanishing below the support degree','026,028','026:20–32; 028:2–19','unproved claim',r'''“Alors la suite spectrale implique” turns the vanishing of local cohomology below $2d$ into the supported-global edge isomorphism and a canonical class. The margin also records that the Kummer class maps to zero in $H^2_Y(X,G_m)$. A proof should preserve exactly which purity statement supplies that vanishing in each of the three cases on 026, rather than treating normality in the divisor case as the full higher-codimension purity theorem.''',C+' N01 (098, immersion duality) **adds** a later operational comparison but does not restate this edge-map argument; the current pages **add** a local-to-global construction under register **MPL_33_098-146 N01** (first-pass lead).',['spectral','support','Kummer','purity','normal'])
add('N29',B,'The point-class basis theorem is promised, with the curve normalization recalled','028,030','028:21–25; 030:2–9','unproved claim',r'''“Il va résulter de le théor.” promises that, for geometrically connected smooth $X$, the point class is a basis of top proper-support cohomology and is independent of the rational point. The curve case is said to have been checked by reduction to a complete curve and the normalization $t(\gamma_{x/X})=1$. The package supplies that normalization and the later trace axioms, but does not write a full proof of the general point-class statement.''',C+' N20 (122, a curve route to singular-coefficient Lefschetz and L-functions) **differs**, while N29 (134,136) **adds** a later abstract trace mechanism; bears on register **MPL_33_098-146 N20/N29** (first-pass leads).',['point','curve','courbe','Kummer','trace'])
add('N30',B,'The relative class diagram needs a proper-support condition','028,030','028:13–17; 030:12–16','own idea',r'''The surviving diagram on 030 maps supported cohomology into a relative $R_!$ term, so one must recover why the support $Y$ is proper over the base, possibly from the preceding properness clause on 028. For the scope test $d=0$, $Y=X=\mathbb A^1_k$ over an algebraically closed field, the nonzero constant section has support all of $X$ and is not a proper-support section. Thus the intended passage cannot be treated as unrestricted forgetting of supports; this does not deny that a zero map between the displayed groups could be written.''',C+' N18 (120, a different nonproper support obstruction) and N30 (136,140,142, ordinary/proper-support distinction) provide **related but different** warnings; this **adds** a cycle-support condition under register **MPL_33_098-146 N30** (first-pass lead).',['support','proper','nonproper','local-to-global','immersion'],'high for the support-family obstruction in the stated example; the manuscript may intend properness inherited from the preceding paragraph')
add('N31',B,'Locally varying dimension is separated from the pure-dimensional trace','032','8–10','unpursued implication',r'''The orientation sheaf is defined “sur ses divers morceaux” when relative dimension varies locally, after which the trace construction explicitly restricts to morphisms pure of dimension $d$. This permits componentwise twists without asserting one uniform global cohomological shift on every non-quasi-compact base. The distinction should be retained when comparing the later unbounded and amplitude statements.''',C+' N31 (138,140) already gives an unreviewed example separating pointwise and uniform bounds, and N32 (140,142) supplies a later amplitude claim; this **adds** the orientation-sheaf locus rather than rederiving that example, beside register **MPL_33_098-146 N30/§76**.',['uniform','pointwise','dimension','component','amplitude'])
add('N32',B,'The four trace normalizations assert both existence and uniqueness','032,034','032:10–12; 034:3–20','unproved claim',r'''The text introduces a canonical $\operatorname{Tr}_f$ and says that base extension, composition, the isomorphism case and the Kummer normalization for $\mathbb P^1$ “caractérisent les hom.” These are substantial existence and uniqueness claims for smooth compactifiable morphisms, beyond the isolated curve calculation on 030. A reconstruction must show that reductions to the normalized cases are independent of the chosen factorization and compatible with the twists.''',C+' N29 (134,136) and N37 (146) **add** intrinsic endomorphism traces and symmetry, but **differ** from this geometric integration map; register **MPL_33_098-146 N29/N37** are first-pass leads and do not settle its characterization.',['trace','Kummer','composition','contraction','normaliz'])
add('N33',B,'Connected scheme fibres do not alone force a trace isomorphism','028,034','028:25; 034:18','own idea',r'''If “fibres conn. non vides” means ordinary scheme-theoretic connectedness, $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb C\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb R$ is a smooth finite étale map of relative dimension zero with connected nonempty fibre, but its geometric fibre has two points. For $A=\mathbb F_3$, the trace on that geometric stalk is the sum $A^2\to A$, not an isomorphism, by the finite étale trace description in [Stacks 03SH](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03SH). The explicit “géom. connexe” on 028 makes it especially useful to check whether 034 uses a convention about geometric fibres or omits a qualification.''',C+' N19 (112,120) discusses local constancy for another formula and N29 (134,136) a different trace; this **adds** a geometric-fibre scope test under register **MPL_33_098-146 N29** (first-pass lead), with no matching earlier counterexample.',['geometric','connected','connexe','trace','fibre'],'high for the conditional two-point stalk test; the intended fibre convention remains unresolved')
add('N34',B,'The local/global trace compatibility is announced in a revised diagram','036','2–33','unproved claim',r'''“Compatibilité avec les hom traces” names the intended conclusion of the heavily revised purity and direct-image diagram. The next sentence says that the relation is easier and more complete in the derived category, so the page itself points beyond its degreewise display. The overwritten degrees and functor labels remain too uncertain to claim that a complete commutative diagram has been recovered mathematically from this leaf.''',C+' N01 (098), N29 (134,136), and N36/N37 (144,146) **add** related exchange, contraction and local-pairing constructions without proving this exact diagram; bears on register **MPL_33_098-146 N01/N29/N36/N37** (all first-pass leads).',['local','global','trace','duality','pairing'])
add('N35',B,'A reconstruction should compare maps before taking cohomology','024,034,036','024:22; 034:7–20; 036:31–33','own idea',r'''A useful route is to build the supported orientation-class map first, then compare its composite with the ambient trace to the trace on the closed subscheme. The divisor normalization and trace transitivity provide initial tests, followed by the regular-sequence independence problem on 024. This turns “s'énonce sous forme plus complète” into a concrete compatibility task, while leaving the construction and commutativity proof open.''',C+' N29 (134,136) and N36/N37 (144,146) **add** later categorical trace/pairing mechanisms; this proposal **differs** from their endomorphism-trace construction and bears on register **MPL_33_098-146 N29/N36/N37** (unreviewed leads).',['orientation','pairing','compatib','trace','local'],'medium: the manuscript supplies normalizations and a target diagram, but their derived realization and descent have not been proved here')

D='III. Derived operations and the duality examples'
add('N36',D,'The flat-resolution promise is taken up in the later package','038','26–32','unproved claim',r'''“dans la suite de ces notes, on explique” explicitly promises a construction of $Lf^*$ by flat rather than projective resolutions. The supplied later report describes flat generators, passage through quasi-isomorphisms and nonflat pullback on scans 100–106, together with a general-topos question and a corrected injective argument. [Stacks 06YV](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/06YV) verifies a modern K-flat construction, but that comparison neither dates the draft nor proves that every compatibility was already checked in it.''',C+' N02–N07 (100,102,104,106): **adds** a reported continuation of the explicit promise; bears on register **MPL_33_098-146 §65/§76/§78** (first-pass leads), not an unqualified completed-proof claim.',['flat','plat','resolution','quasi-isom','pullback'])
add('N37',D,'Flat pullback and finite Tor dimension are different assertions','038,044','038:12–32; 044:2','unpursued implication',r'''Scan 038 separates exact pullback in the flat case from the “Tor-dim. finie” condition used to preserve $D^+$ and $D^b$, while 044 says that the constant-coefficient étale-topos morphisms under consideration are flat. That statement concerns the functor, not a conclusion that every module over $\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z$ is perfect. The earlier supplied notes already contain coefficient-module and perfect-complex examples that make this distinction, so the present contribution is the exact earlier manuscript locus.''',C+' N08/N10 (108,112) and N25 (126–132): **adds** the flat-topos antecedent and **restates** the need to separate hypotheses; register **MPL_33_098-146 N08/N25** are unreviewed leads whose examples are not rederived here.',['Tor','flat','plat','perfect','coefficient'])
add('N38',D,'The dismissive margin concerns right-derived pullback','038','34','reading question',r'''“Rien n'empêche de construire un dérivé droit, $Rf^*$” explicitly concerns the starred inverse-image functor, followed by the judgment that it has no interest. It must not be read as a dismissal of $Rf_*$, which the surrounding pages use centrally. The margin records a methodological preference; it does not supply a theorem about when every possible right derivation of an additive functor is useful.''',C+' N06 (106, corrected injective adjunction argument) **differs** from this marginal judgment; this **adds** a notation-sensitive comparison point to README **S19**, with no matching result in '+R+'.',['injective','right derived','pullback','adjunction'])
add('N39',D,'Derived Hom and the spectral sequences need their convergence scope','040','2–23','unproved claim',r'''The boxed relation between global derived Hom and $R\Gamma_X$ of internal derived Hom is followed by two Ext spectral-sequence displays, the second annotated “pris terme à terme.” The page does not state all boundedness and convergence hypotheses needed to pass among those expressions. Its complex-letter G/C uncertainty is independent of this mathematical scope question.''',C+' N05 (104, finite-dimensional/global hyper-Tor qualifications) and N34 (144, a separate H-zero issue) **add** related cautions but do not settle the current spectral sequences; bears on register **MPL_33_098-146 §76** (first-pass comparison lead).',['Hom','spectral','bounded','conver','hyper'])
add('N40',D,'Derived composition requires compatible compactification comparisons','042','2–20','unproved claim',r'''The “Reformulation des propriétés connues” asserts derived composition and compatibility with base extension for compactifiable maps. [Stacks 0F7J](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0F7J) proves a corresponding canonical composition isomorphism for finite-type separated maps of quasi-compact, quasi-separated schemes and explicitly checks independence of choices. This is a precise comparison for the coherence missing from the short assertion, not a reason to identify every displayed equality with a strictly equal chain-level construction.''',C+' N11/N12 (112,114) **add** a later comparison whose functoriality is explicitly left to verify; the present page **adds** its earlier structural input under register **MPL_33_098-146 §65/§76** (first-pass leads).',['compactif','composition','functorial','fonctor','base change'])
add('N41',D,'The unbounded extension replaces its hypothesis on the same page','042 → 042','22–33','withdrawn in the same text',r'''The initial “$f$ est à fibres de dim bornée” is cancelled and replaced by “$Y$ est quasi compact” before the extension to unbounded $D(X)$ and the three boundedness-preservation assertions. The justification still appeals to finite cohomological dimension for the proper compactification; [Stacks 095U](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/095U) supplies the proper fibre-dimension bound, and [0G2A](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0G2A) explains the resulting boundedness of $Rf_!$ for separated finite-type morphisms between quasi-compact, quasi-separated schemes, with a torsion coefficient ring for the unbounded extension. The revision changes a hypothesis of this assertion, rather than retracting the whole compact-support construction.''',C+' N09 (110, ordinary nonproper relative dimension) **differs**, while N30–N33 (136,138,140,142) **add** later compact-support/Tor-bound questions; bears on register **MPL_33_098-146 N30/§65/§76** (first-pass leads), with no contradiction of the later open ordinary-image question.',['unbounded','quasi','dimension','relative','compact'])
add('N42',D,'A constructed derived comparison can be checked on cohomology sheaves','044','6–19','unproved claim',r'''The page constructs the derived base-change arrow and, when $f$ is proper, concludes that it is an isomorphism “puisque on sait que les $(*_i)$ le sont.” This is the cohomology-sheaf detection step after a comparison morphism exists, and [Stacks 095T](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/095T) is the corresponding proper torsion comparison. It does not by itself check that different resolution-level constructions give the same functorial morphism.''',C+' N12 (114) **restates the distinction** by separately flagging derived functoriality; this **adds** an earlier instance under register **MPL_33_098-146 §65/§76** (first-pass leads).',['base change','functorial','cohomology sheaves','proper'])
add('N43',D,'Duality is proposed as a way to transport finiteness','048','2–20','unproved claim',r'''“On retrouve le th. de finitude de Artin” follows the identification of the dual of compact-support cohomology with ordinary cohomology of $DF$. Over a general base, the page adds local-constancy assumptions on the compact-support direct images and proposes a constructibility consequence for ordinary direct images of the dual. A reconstruction must state the inherited duality and coefficient hypotheses before treating this as an unrestricted nonproper finiteness theorem.''',C+' N01 (098) **restates** the exchange principle, while N19 (112,120) and N30 (136,140,142) **differ** in their finiteness/projection tasks; the current example **adds** a concrete intended application under register **MPL_33_098-146 N01/N30** (first-pass leads).',['Artin','finit','duality','ordinary','constructib'])
add('N44',D,'The live and cancelled support signs cannot be normalized together','048','14–18','reading question',r'''The two main right-hand $R$ expressions have cancelled $!$ marks, but the intervening parenthesis still says “les $R^{-i}_!f_*(DF)$.” The later context's careful distinction between ordinary and proper-support operations makes this discrepancy mathematically material. It remains a post-freeze reading/scope question about whether the parenthesis was overlooked or had a distinct intended claim.''',C+' N18 (120) and N30 (136,140,142) **add** support distinctions but provide no matching line that resolves this one; bears on register **MPL_33_098-146 N30** (first-pass lead), with no transcription change.',['proper-support','ordinary','support','duality','direct image'])
add('N45',D,'The coefficient sheaf in the relative duality display has a base-index question','048','12–18','reading question',r'''The relative displays pair $R^i_!f_*F$ with the literal coefficient sheaf “$(\mathbf Z/n\mathbf Z)_X$”. If the direct image lives on the base $S$, this target index needs explanation or collation with the intended formula; the same question is separate from the cancelled support signs. The context does not license replacing the frozen $X$ by an expected base letter.''',C+' N34 (144) and N39 (146) record **different** type/domain anomalies in later derived formulas; this **adds** a relative-duality indexing question to README **S19**, with no same-point result in '+R+'.',['coefficient','base','mismatch','Hom','type'])
add('N46',D,'The final partial-range assertion preserves the reversal of degree','048','22','unpursued implication',r'''“$i\leq i_0$ … $j\geq2d-i_0$” proposes transferring a one-sided range through the written duality relation $j=2d-i$. The direction is consistent with that reversal, but the hypotheses needed to convert the duality into local constancy or constructibility still have to be carried along. No claim outside the stated range follows from this last sentence alone.''',C+' N32/N33 (138–142) **differ** by treating a Tor-amplitude shift and its homological indexing; the present page **adds** another range-sensitive statement beside register **MPL_33_098-146 N30/§76** (first-pass leads).',['degree','amplitude','homological','range','bound'])
add('N47',D,'The conclusion is revised from finiteness to constructibility','048 → 048','20','withdrawn in the same text',r'''The sentence beginning “Donc dans ces cas” deletes “finitude,” inserts “constructibilité,” and cancels parts of the ensuing functor expression. This is an explicit revision of the wording and notation of the relative consequence, not a demonstrated denial of the earlier field-case finiteness assertion on the same page. Its mathematical reconstruction should start from the surviving conclusion and the hypotheses above it.''',C+' N30 (136,140,142) **differs** in its finite-Tor requirement and N36 (144,146) in its cancelled local pairing; this **adds** a separate same-page revision under register **MPL_33_098-146 §65/N30/N36** (first-pass leads).',['finitude','constructib','cancel','withdraw','revision'])
